stages.template:
Extend and improve the accepted ontology specification for the {domain_name} domain through NeOn ontology-reuse principles. Explore reuse by constructing a small, provisional fragment of the current domain ontology that applies suitable existing vocabulary or modeling patterns.

ACCEPTED SPECIFICATION:
{previous_step_content}

DOMAIN DOCUMENT:
{domain_description}

REUSE GUIDANCE:
{reuse_example_desc}

FEW-SHOT EXAMPLES — guidance, not accepted mappings or source evidence:
{few_shot_02}

Identify the domain concepts, relationships, and requirements that would benefit from reuse. Prioritize existing ontologies and vocabularies that model the current domain or a relevant part of it. General-purpose ontologies and vocabularies are also eligible when their terms meet a specific need in this domain; familiarity or widespread use alone is not sufficient justification.

For each reuse candidate, identify the relevant concept or relationship in the supplied document or specification, the candidate ontology or vocabulary and its applicable terms or modeling pattern, the proposed mapping or use, and the semantic justification for accepting or rejecting it. Explain any differences in scope or meaning and any adaptation needed.

Apply the suitable candidates to a small, coherent part of the current domain model. Identify the relevant classes and properties, then express their proposed structure as conceptual subject-relation-object triples. Explain which terms or patterns are reused and which are proposed local modeling choices. Tie each part of the fragment to the supplied document or accepted specification. Distinguish source-supported information from provisional modeling choices, and keep uncertain mappings explicit. Use descriptive labels when a term IRI is uncertain. The fragment is a provisional modeling proposal, not an additional source of domain requirements.

Represent the document's complexity and hierarchy where justified. Treat reuse guidance and examples as optional references and retain only suggestions relevant to the current domain and accepted requirements. Do not force a term whose semantics differ, invent external ontology names or term IRIs, claim a live ontology search, or override accepted requirements with an example. If no suitable candidate can be identified for a domain requirement, state that limitation without presenting a local proposal as verified reuse. Preserve the accepted specification and return the extended specification, domain-grounded reuse decisions, and the provisional domain model fragment. Generate only the content explicitly requested in this prompt, without adding anything beyond the requested scope.

Using the full accepted ontology requirements and the complete document, write {cq_count} distinct competency questions. Keep the NeOn coverage objective: simple and compositional information needs, the whole source rather than a snippet, no paraphrase-only duplicates, and no target ontology-size constraint. If the source cannot support the requested count, return only supported questions and explain the shortfall in an optional note; never fabricate evidence.

ACCEPTED REQUIREMENTS:
{previous_step_content}

DOCUMENT PARAGRAPHS (authoritative IDs and source text):
{document_paragraphs}

FEW-SHOT EXAMPLES — only format guidance:
{few_shot_03}

Return exactly one JSON object between ###start_output### and ###end_output###:
{"cqs":[{"id":"CQ1","question":"a source-grounded question","evidence":[{"paragraph_id":"P0001","text":"an exact nonempty quote from that paragraph"}]}]}
Use stable unique IDs CQ1, CQ2, ... . Every CQ needs one or more relevant evidence entries. Copy text exactly from the paragraph identified by paragraph_id; do not paraphrase quotations, invent paragraph IDs, use an example as evidence, or invent unsupported questions. Store all relevant supporting paragraphs for a CQ when it spans paragraphs. Optional note is allowed; prose outside the JSON is not.

For every accepted CQ, extract the entities, object/data properties and axioms needed to answer it. A CQ can require multiple elements and triples. Cover all CQs, preserve source qualifications and disambiguate terms with the cited source; do not invent elements merely to enlarge the ontology.

ACCEPTED CQ JSON:
{competency_questions}

SOURCE PARAGRAPHS:
{document_paragraphs}

FEW-SHOT EXAMPLES — format guidance only:
{few_shot_04}

Return exactly one JSON object between ###start_output### and ###end_output###:
{"elements":[{"id":"https://example.org/ontology/Term","kind":"class","label":"Term","cq_ids":["CQ1"]}],"triples":[{"subject":"https://example.org/ontology/Term","predicate":"http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type","object":"http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class","cq_ids":["CQ1"]}]}
Every element id must be a stable absolute IRI. kind must be class, object_property, or data_property. Every element and triple needs a nonempty cq_ids array of existing accepted CQ IDs; use only the accepted CQ IDs. Include all necessary classes and properties in elements. Triples use absolute IRIs for resources and a Turtle-quoted literal for literal objects. Optional rationale strings are allowed. Reuse IDs consistently in later steps.

Transform the full previous extraction into a complete conceptual subject–relation–object model. Cover all accepted entities, properties and axioms, not a snippet. Avoid wrong inverse relations, unintended class-hierarchy cycles, inappropriate domains/ranges, and unjustified transitivity.

EXTRACTION:
{previous_step_content}

ACCEPTED CQ JSON:
{competency_questions}

ELEMENT CATALOG:
{element_catalog}

FEW-SHOT EXAMPLES — format guidance only:
{few_shot_05}

Return one JSON object between ###start_output### and ###end_output### with keys elements and triples, preserving the stage 04 schema. elements: {id: absolute IRI, kind: class|object_property|data_property, label: string, cq_ids: existing CQ IDs}. triples: {subject: absolute IRI, predicate: absolute IRI, object: absolute IRI or Turtle-quoted literal, cq_ids: existing CQ IDs}. Return the entire conceptual model. Preserve stable element IDs and every relevant CQ association; add only source-supported missing elements. Do not replace existing IDs or change their class/property kind.

Extend the conceptual model using relevant domain keywords and appropriate reuse.
KEYWORDS: {keywords}
REUSE GUIDANCE: {reuse_example_desc}

CURRENT CONCEPTUAL MODEL:
{previous_step_content}

ACCEPTED CQ JSON:
{competency_questions}

CURRENT ELEMENT CATALOG:
{element_catalog}

SOURCE PARAGRAPHS:
{document_paragraphs}

FEW-SHOT EXAMPLES — guidance, never evidence:
{few_shot_06}

Add missing entities, properties and axioms only when they improve source-supported completeness, interoperability or CQ answering. Avoid size-driven additions, unjustified strong axioms and duplicates. Return ONLY additions as one JSON object between ###start_output### and ###end_output### with elements and triples using the stage 04 schema: each element has absolute IRI id, kind, label, nonempty existing cq_ids; each triple has subject, predicate, object, nonempty existing cq_ids. Reuse stable IDs. Existing elements may be listed only to add CQ associations; do not change their kind. If no justified additions are needed, return {"elements":[],"triples":[]}.

Review the accumulated conceptual model again for remaining source-supported gaps in entities, relations, axioms and CQ coverage.
KEYWORDS: {keywords}
REUSE GUIDANCE: {reuse_example_desc}

CURRENT MODEL INCLUDING STAGE 06 ADDITIONS:
{previous_step_content}

ACCEPTED CQ JSON:
{competency_questions}

CURRENT ELEMENT CATALOG:
{element_catalog}

SOURCE PARAGRAPHS:
{document_paragraphs}

FEW-SHOT EXAMPLES — format guidance only:
{few_shot_07}

Add only semantically justified content not already represented. Avoid duplicate triples, arbitrary size growth, incorrect inverses, cycles or unjustified property characteristics. Return ONLY additions as one JSON object between ###start_output### and ###end_output### with elements and triples using the stage 04 schema. Every element and triple retains nonempty existing cq_ids. Element id is a stable absolute IRI and kind is class, object_property, or data_property; never change an existing element's kind. If no supported additions remain, return {"elements":[],"triples":[]}.

Serialize the COMPLETE conceptual model and accumulated additions as valid RDF/OWL Turtle.

FULL CONCEPTUAL MODEL:
{previous_step_content}

ACCEPTED CQ JSON:
{competency_questions}

ELEMENT CATALOG:
{element_catalog}

FEW-SHOT EXAMPLES — syntax guidance only:
{few_shot_08}

Define all classes, object properties and data properties clearly; retain justified axioms, subclass/type statements, domain/range semantics, human-readable labels and useful descriptions. Use consistent prefixes and stable absolute IRIs matching element_catalog; never rename or change the kind of catalog entries. Preserve the entire conceptual model, not a snippet.

Retain CQ provenance as annotations: declare <https://example.org/yonsei/relatedCQ> a owl:AnnotationProperty. For EVERY catalog element, emit one <https://example.org/yonsei/relatedCQ> literal annotation for every cq_ids entry (e.g. "CQ1"). These are provenance annotations, not domain properties or domain assertions. Include every catalog element and all its accepted CQ links in the full ontology.
Return the complete valid Turtle ONLY between ###start_turtle### and ###end_turtle### markers.
